# Build context hygiene. Both images use the repository root as their context
# (the frontend needs the pnpm workspace and `web/shared`), so this file serves
# the API and the Next apps at once.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Never in an image
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Secrets. `.env.example` is documentation and is kept; a real `.env` must never
# be baked into a layer, where it would survive every later deletion.
#
# The `**/` forms matter more than the root ones: the Next apps keep `web/*/.env.local`,
# and Next inlines `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` at build time — so a host file saying
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000` would be compiled into the browser
# bundle and shipped. A root-only pattern would not have caught it.
.env
**/.env
.env.local
**/.env.local
.env.*.local
**/.env.*.local

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Host-installed dependencies — the single most important exclusion.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The host `node_modules` trees are pnpm symlink farms pointing at a store outside
# the context. Copying them in produces a build that resolves to dangling links and
# fails in ways that look like source errors. The images install their own.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# The host virtualenv, same reasoning.
.venv
**/.venv

# Stale build output. Let the image build these; a cached `.next` from the host can
# reference host paths.
.next
**/.next
out
**/out

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Local state and noise
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
*.db
*.sqlite3
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc

# Docs, specs and design notes: useful in the repository, dead weight in a layer.
docs
.trae
*.md
